event('236-2.10-ii','carried as background without fault found',
      'Read the H2 vanishing condition, semicontinuity reduction and infinitesimal smoothness criterion. The standard deformation obstruction for a line bundle lies in H2 of its square-zero ideal; the assertion uses proper flat cohomology and the representability assumptions in force. These deformation and cohomology theorems are background here, not a complete independent proof.',
      '236, p. 236, proposition 2.10(ii) and explanation')
event('236-2.10-iii','targeted proof check',
      'With the standard tangent-space identification T_e Pic=H1(X,O_X), the dimension of the local group at its rational identity is bounded by embedding dimension. Equality persists over an algebraic closure and is equivalent there to regularity at the identity; translations then give smoothness of the whole group. Conversely smoothness gives equality. The tangent identification and Cartier theorem in characteristic zero are background inputs.',
      '236, p. 236, proposition 2.10(iii) and explanation')
event('236-3.1','targeted proof check',
      'Over the algebraic closure the reduced identity component H is semiabelian by the no-additive-component hypothesis. The finite commutative quotient G/H is killed by an integer m, and [m] on H is surjective; consequently the scheme-theoretic image of [m] on G is exactly H. Schematic images commute with this flat field extension, so the image descends to k and is geometrically reduced. This checks the descent argument conditional on the semiabelian structure and finite-quotient facts. The second printed G_k must mean G over the algebraic closure; corrected French restores the bar. The grammatical erratum is left in its separate section.',
      '236, p. 236, proposition 3.1 and proof')
event('236-3.2-normal-counterexample','independent re-proof',
      'Let k=F_3(a), with a transcendental, and X the plane cubic y^2 z=x^3+a z^3. On z=1 the only nonsmooth closed point has ideal (y,x^3+a)=(y), so its dimension-one local ring is regular; every other affine point and the point [0:1:0] are smooth. Thus X is regular and proper. Over an algebraic closure, shifting x by a cube root of a gives the cusp y^2 z=u^3. Its normalization is P1, and the quotient of local units K[[t]]^*/K[[t^2,t^3]]^* is G_a (the coefficient of t); hence Pic^0 of this geometric curve is G_a, not proper. Picard base change and the normalization exact sequence are the background inputs. The connected Picard group of X is smooth since H2 of a curve vanishes. Therefore it cannot contain a closed abelian subscheme with the same underlying set: reducedness would force that subscheme to be the whole nonproper group. This independently justifies adding geometric normality.',
      '236, p. 236, corollary 3.2, missing geometric-normality hypothesis')
